# What must not reach a Docker build context.
#
# The server's image is built from the repository root, because the Dockerfile
# needs the whole apps/server tree and cannot reach above its own context. That
# makes this file load-bearing rather than an optimisation.
#
# Build output is the reason. `dotnet publish --no-restore` reads
# obj/project.assets.json, and a copy of that file written on a developer's
# machine names an absolute NuGet path — C:\Users\someone\.nuget\packages — that
# does not exist inside the container. The publish then fails inside
# FallbackPackagePathResolver with a stack trace that says nothing about the
# real cause. Without this the image builds only on a checkout nobody has ever
# built locally, which is to say on CI and never on the machine of the person
# who changed something.

# .NET
**/bin/
**/obj/

# Node
**/node_modules/
**/out/
**/release/
**/dist/

# A web client build left over from a local run. The image builds its own, in
# the web stage, and one copied in here would be whatever this machine last
# built rather than what this commit says.
apps/server/src/Foxfire.Api/wwwroot/

# Never a secret, however careful the Dockerfile is
**/.env
**/*.pfx

# Nothing here is needed to compile or run anything
.git/
.github/
.claude/
**/.vs/
**/.vscode/
**/.idea/
**/*.md
fixtures/
